1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:04,650
Relay helps people have the conversations they avoid—

2
00:00:04,650 --> 00:00:10,250
asking for money, arranging a meeting,
or declining politely—

3
00:00:10,250 --> 00:00:14,050
without giving up control.

4
00:00:14,538 --> 00:00:17,850
A user privately says what they want.

5
00:00:17,850 --> 00:00:21,350
Relay understands the goal,
drafts a clear message,

6
00:00:21,350 --> 00:00:24,050
and waits for approval
before anything is shared.

7
00:00:24,527 --> 00:00:26,150
Watch a real flow.

8
00:00:26,150 --> 00:00:29,150
I want to set up a meeting tomorrow.

9
00:00:29,150 --> 00:00:33,650
Relay turns that into a clean message,
I pick a tone, and I approve it.

10
00:00:33,650 --> 00:00:36,250
Then I share a secure invite link.

11
00:00:36,711 --> 00:00:39,650
The other person joins and replies.

12
00:00:39,650 --> 00:00:45,450
Relay keeps private notes private—
only I see what I actually typed.

13
00:00:45,450 --> 00:00:51,650
When we settle on a time, the goal updates live:
date, time, and status—

14
00:00:51,650 --> 00:00:55,500
proposed until both sides agree,
then agreed.

15
00:00:55,999 --> 00:01:01,100
Nothing is treated as a commitment
until it is confirmed.

16
00:01:01,100 --> 00:01:03,550
Silence is not yes.

17
00:01:03,550 --> 00:01:06,300
A suggestion is not an agreement.

18
00:01:06,746 --> 00:01:13,350
I built Relay with Codex—the conversation state model,
invite flow, and real-time chat,

19
00:01:13,350 --> 00:01:19,200
and the privacy boundary
between intent and shared messages.

20
00:01:19,200 --> 00:01:26,200
GPT-5.6, through Codex, helped shape the goal framing,
trust rules, and UX decisions.

21
00:01:26,200 --> 00:01:32,450
Live drafting uses reliable model routing,
but humans stay in the approval loop.

22
00:01:32,957 --> 00:01:36,250
Relay—say it better.

23
00:01:36,250 --> 00:01:39,300
Try it at relay.durgaai.com.
